We've investigated parametric seeding of a microresonator frequency comb using a pump laser with electro-optic-modulated sidebands. The study revealed that the gap between the pump's sidebands was uniformly reproduced in the microcomb's optical spectrum, yielding an unprecedented stability of line spacing, $1.6\times10^{-13}$, at 1-second intervals. Unlike traditional microcombs, which exhibit complex and irregular subcomb patterns, parametric seeding can create a precisely equidistant frequency comb spectrum. This phenomenon can provide a precise reference frequency for advanced optical sensing applications. Notably, we observed the creation of a new gap due to interference between the pump and the second subcomb, known as the'shadow gap,' in addition to the expected gap created by the pump's sidebands. This research presents a new finding that could contribute to the development of novel microcomb-based technologies. We expect this technique to have a significant impact on the creation of stable and precise optical frequency comb sources. See the complete article for further details. (Figure is illustrated in the Appendix) [Figure: Illustration of the relationship between the pump and shadow gaps in a parametrically seeded microcomb.] \end{abstract} 

